<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Is DevOps a good career? Yes — for the right person, and the honest 2026 answer depends less on the field
    itself than on which market you enter and whether you can accept the on-call reality most course marketing
    leaves out.</strong>
    Product companies and Global Capability Centres are growing DevOps, SRE, and platform-engineering hiring faster
    than general IT roles and paying two to three times more than IT-services DevOps work at the same experience
    level. Getting into that better-paid tier takes more than a cloud certification — it takes one real, documented
    piece of infrastructure work and honesty about the on-call trade-off. That combination — a genuine
    <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> built on proof, not badges — is what actually turns the DevOps label
    into real <strong>high income opportunities</strong> and a path toward <strong>earlier financial freedom</strong>,
    not the certificate by itself.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Yes, DevOps is a good career for the right person in 2026 — but the field splits sharply by employer type: product companies and GCCs pay two to three times more than IT-services firms for the same experience level.</li>
      <li>Fresher pay ranges by employer: Rs 3.5-5.5 LPA at IT-services firms, Rs 6-10 LPA at mid-tier product companies with a real portfolio, Rs 9-18 LPA into a strong unicorn or GCC bench.</li>
      <li>Roughly 7 in 10 DevOps and SRE roles carry some on-call rotation — this is the decision factor most "is DevOps good" content skips, and it should be asked about directly in every interview.</li>
      <li>AI is automating routine log analysis, alert correlation, and boilerplate pipeline code, not the judgment layer — deciding if an alert is real, making infrastructure trade-offs, and owning an incident end to end. Rising infrastructure complexity from AI adoption itself is increasing demand, not shrinking it.</li>
      <li><strong>Building a real home-lab portfolio and choosing your specialisation deliberately — platform engineering, SRE, or cloud-cost depth — is what turns DevOps into a genuine high-value skill portfolio and earlier financial freedom, not certificate collection alone.</strong></li>
      <li>Test your own fit with one real, documented pipeline or infrastructure project before committing real money to an expensive bootcamp or certification bundle.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

